Z||000009||FieroSTETZ||12-23-2000||12:49 PM||||what do you mean you can't stand to drive it in traffic- a clutch is a clutch, right? (wrong? tell me!) im looking for a clutch that is not going to slip on hard launches- i would rather my tires slip than my clutch. would the kevlar clutch do that? also, how much is an aluminum flywheel, and is it really worth it? I am on a serious budget, but want the best clutch. does the aluminum flywheel do anything to help the reliability, or durability of the engine?||||reg||1
Z||000010||Phil||12-23-2000||02:16 PM||||A 4 puck clutch is basicly a racing clutch, it's either engaged or it's not. Ya don't do smoooooth take-offs but they sure do hold.||||reg||1
Z||000011||fieroshop||12-23-2000||02:58 PM||||Fierostetz, the best way to dicuss all the pro,s and con,s of the clutch is for you to call me. My tel # is on my website. There is to much information for me to post on the forum. Call me on Tuesday as I am not in the shop until then.<BR>George<P>------------------<BR> <BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fieroshop_sig_1.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||88formula||12-23-2000||04:02 PM||||I have the fieroshop kevlar clutch but I have not driven the car yet with this clutch because my 3.4-liter engine is still in the machine shop. When I got the clutch I noticed that the quality of that clutch is top notch. George Miller claims that his setup has one of the highest clamp loads @ 2400 lbs. George Miller is a great guy. I have talked to him many times.<P>I am going to get the aluminum flywheel for my setup also. The aluminum flywheel is half the weight of your cast iron flywheel. The reduced weight lowers the reciprocating mass that the engine has to overcome before any power reaches the rear wheels. This increases horsepower and allows the engine to rev quicker. You will notice stronger acceleration. Any reciprocating weight that the engine must get into motion sucks up horsepower. It is one of the only modifications were you gain horsepower and lose nothing in return. It has the same effect that lighter wheels, driveshafts, and any other drivetrain part has on reciprocating mass. The lower the reciprocating weight is the more of the engines available horsepower is used to propel the car instead of overcoming reciprocating weight.<P>Think about how much effort it would take you to turn a 300-lb. wheel compared to a 100-lb. wheel. Which would be easier?  <P><BR> Here is a guy who post here that has this flywheel and he sums it up great. <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/003566.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/003566.html</A>  ||||reg||1
Z||000013||Tina||12-23-2000||09:10 PM||||UHH, remember that one.<BR>This is the answer I got after asking my BF what clutch to buy.<BR>LUK, if that brand is good enough to be in a VIPER than it's good enough for your Fiero.<BR> ...   A man of few words  ...<P>Clutch rep set includes clutch disc,throwout-<BR>bearing,pressure plate and pilot bushing or bearing where applicable.<BR>OH, the alignment tool for an extra $<BR>You might have to call around a bit, but not <BR>..THAT.. hard to find.<BR>Retail price is around $200<P>The LUK was orginally put in when I first got the Fiero,and might I say holding up great with the new and bigger engine/383. Can't say the same though for the tranny.<P>Good Luck<BR>Tina   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000014||88formula||12-23-2000||10:12 PM||||383! Man what the?||||reg||1
Z||000015||rockcrawl||12-23-2000||10:19 PM||||In regards to aluminum flywheels:<BR> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>. Any reciprocating weight that the engine must get into motion sucks up horsepower. It is one of the only modifications were you gain horsepower and lose nothing in return.<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>That's not entirely true for every aplication. For rockcrawling, where being able to go as <B>slow</B> as possible is the name of the game, the heavier the flywheel, the better. When you're idling along with 60:1 gear reduction, and a tire comes upon an obstacle, you need the rotating mass of the flywheel to prevent the engine from stalling without goosing the throttle. I agree that a lighter flywheel can be advantageous for a Fiero, but I don't think it's correct to say you gain horsepower and lose nothing in return.<P>Websters definition of a flywheel:<BR>a <B>heavy</B> wheel for regulating the speed and uniformity of motion of the machine to which it is attached.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000016||Tina||12-23-2000||11:05 PM||||<P>   88formula<BR>                383! Man what the?<P><BR>I think my Bumpersticker explains best<P>NOT JUST BOYS DRIVE BAD A$$ TOYS<P><BR>Tina   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000017||88formula||12-24-2000||01:49 AM||||I didn't know that the fiero had a 60-1 gear ratio. <P>Webster’s definition is old school thought that applies to older one cylinder engines that need the reciprocating weight to get it through three of the four cycles in an internal combustion engine. Those engines were some of the first engines ever made. Since when have you seen a big heavy flywheel on a modern lawnmower engine or some other smaller one cylinder engine to keep it going. I find it hard to believe that an off road vehicle that usually has a big v8 with tons of low-end torque and a 60-1 gear ratio will stall out if it comes upon an obstacle. Am aluminum flywheel would be useless in such a vehicle anyway because of the great gear reduction it has and slow speeds it travels.<P>Manufacturers use cast iron flywheels because they are cheap and reliable.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||Bruce||12-24-2000||04:37 PM||||All I know about aluminum is that the defective clutch pedals were made out of it!||||reg||1
Z||000019||88formula||12-24-2000||04:52 PM||||Bruce, what does that mean, that everything made out of aluminum is defective junk? <P>Come on man think!<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000020||rockcrawl||12-24-2000||06:04 PM||||Hey 88formula man, I wasn't trying to start any flame wars or anything. I was just tying to make the point that there <B>is</B> something to be lost by lightening the flywheel. Momentum (mass x velocity)of the engine will be lost in every case, and in some cases it will be detrimental to performance.  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>I find it hard to believe that an off road vehicle that usually has a big v8 with tons of low-end torque and a 60-1 gear ratio will stall out if it comes upon an obstacle<HR></BLOCKQUOTE><BR>Obviously you have never done any serious crawling. One of my Jeeps has a TBI chevy V8 with 40:1 low and the other has a  Nissan turbo diesel with 60:1 low. You'd be surprised how easy it is to stall them. I would not even think of using a lightweight flywheel in either one. I know this forum is not about off-roading, but like I said, I was just trying to make a point. And since we're talking about lawn mowers, take the blade off your two stroke Lawn-boy sometime and see how it runs. It will run, but it will idle like a dirt bike. The blade (flywheel) does a lot for regulating the speed and uniformity of motion as Webster would say.<P>Again, I agree that an aluminum flywheel would be great for a Fiero. I have talked (via email) to George about a custom aluminum flywheel for my 4.9 conversion. When I resume work on it, I will probably buy one. Sorry for the off-topic nonsense!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000021||88formula||12-25-2000||11:34 AM||||Rockcrawl, I'm not flaming you and this is not a flame war. Like you I am merely arguing my opinion or point with a little humor thrown in. Flame wars are when people get negative and go back and forth "mine is better, no mine is better". I guess I should have put "j.k." after that first comment, SORRY. <P>I have a truck but I don't do any serious off road driving. I just use it for work. I never thought about the blade on a lawnmower engine acting like a flywheel. I always thought of that part as the engine load. Kind of like when you put your engine in gear, but good point though. I can see why an aluminum flywheel would not be practical in a truck. Trucks usually have big heavy wheels and drive trains and they usually weigh in at over two tons. Trucks and sports cars are made for two very different purposes anyway. <P>Here is a quote from Fidanza flywheels Corp "Using a lightweight, aluminum flywheel is a race proven way to improve the performance of any street or race car. Aluminum flywheels have been used for many years by the world's most elite cars to improve both useable horsepower and extend engine life”. “An aluminum flywheel is much lighter than obsolete and heavy factory flywheels. Most of our flywheels are less than half the weight of their steel counterpart”. <P>Here is a quote about a point I didn’t mention, “Engine life is also increased by using a Fidanza Flywheel. Since our flywheel is so much lighter there is much less stress on the main bearings”. <P>Source <A HREF="http://www.aluminumflywheels.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.aluminumflywheels.com</A>  <P>Here is another quote, “MORE ACCELERATION!! For maximum acceleration there is no substitute for an aluminum flywheel. The decrease in rotating mass allows maximum engine revs to be reached very quickly, resulting in a startling decrease in acceleration time. In some racing organizations, aluminum flywheels have actually been outlawed because they give such a huge advantage.<BR>RELIABLE AND SAFE!! An aluminum flywheel is an ideal performance modification because it has no disadvantages. You lose nothing in reliability, safety, or derivability on the street. What you gain is neck-snapping acceleration from your car”.<P>Source <A HREF="http://www.paeco.com/fly1.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.paeco.com/fly1.html</A>  <P>One more, “Because of their lightweight, energy normally used to turn the heavy stock steel flywheel is now used to turn your drive wheels! In addition, the reduced mass allows the engine to rev faster and get you into your power band more quickly. For turbo cars this is even a greater advantage, allowing the turbo to kick in faster since the engine is revving more quickly! Our flywheels are the perfect choice for 1/4mile racing, autocross, and road race events.<BR>Horsepower gains can vary widely with aluminum flywheels depending on what has been done to the vehicle. Gains averaging from 15 to 30 horsepower at the drive wheels can be realized”.<P>Source <A HREF="http://www.muellerfabricators.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.muellerfabricators.com</A>  <P>Again no flame wars intended. <P> <P><p>[This message has been edited by 88formula (edited 12-25-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000022||rodmcneill||12-25-2000||12:03 PM||||On these aluminum flywheels what material is the ring gear made of? What price for a Fiero aluminum flywheel?||||reg||5
Z||000023||rockcrawl||12-25-2000||12:09 PM||||OK, I agree with all that. And as far as Fieros go, I think lightweight flywheels are a great performance upgrade. <P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/rockcrawlsig.jpg"> ||||reg||7
Z||000024||rockcrawl||12-25-2000||12:14 PM||||rodmcneill,<BR> Every one I've seen uses a steel ring gear and a steel friction surface. I think Fieroshop sells the complete set (clutch press plate, and flywheel) for $950.<P>Jon<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000025||Shaft||12-25-2000||05:03 PM||||I'll Agree with Tina on this matter.<P>I work for LuK and part of my job was to test clutches and discs (on a test stand) and I have to say that they build a nice product. LuK clutches go in everything from the huge Dodge trucks to Vettes, Vipers, Aston Martins, Jaguars, Porches...etc.<P>I wouldn't be able to give you a number to call since i worked at the plant and not at the aftermarket center, but I can tell you that the RepSet includes everything you'd need to replace your clutch. Talk to the guys in the aftermarket and tell them what you drive and what you want. They should be able to hook you up. <BR> <A HREF="http://www.lukclutch.com/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.lukclutch.com/</A> <P>I think this is the site you'll need for info.||||reg||1
Z||000026||Cooter||12-25-2000||05:35 PM||||I have a LUK clutch set in my 3.4 GT and it has only slipped one time since I have been driving it. I believe that it slipped because I had not given it enough time to get "broke in" because it has been fine since then. However, I would really like one of Fieroshops aluminum flywheels and clutches.||||reg||1
Z||000027||Tina||12-25-2000||09:42 PM||||RE LUK's<BR>With my 84 I had a choice of what size of<BR>Clutchdisk/pressure plate I wanted ?<P>So don't forget to ask<P>HEY, the more surface, the better the grab, right?<BR>Tina<BR>BTW Thanks Shaft for backing me up there,<BR>well actually Eric.Always his first choice when it comes to clutch-replacment.<BR>||||reg||1
